21
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.840
especially for those of you not familiar with what we do.

22
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.320
Tessl is building tools to help

23
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.320
you build your software factory.

24
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.120
Now, we've been doing this for ourselves for a couple of years already,

25
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.200
and one of the things we've learned is that building

26
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.200
a software factory isn't just about building the plumbing.

27
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.920
It's actually quite straightforward to build the necessary steps

28
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.040
to take an issue to an agent, to generate code and create a PR.

29
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.280
What's really difficult is for that PR to actually have code

30
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.320
you can trust, code you're willing to merge into your code base.

31
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.120
And so with that in mind, Tessl's learned along the way that in order

32
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:59.560
to build trustworthy code with an agent, you need to have the right context.

33
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.720
And so for a long time, Tessl has worked on managing context,

34
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.280
building tools to inventory the context that you have in your repo

35
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.800
to evaluate how good it is, and make sure that you're

36
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.800
always improving the context that's in that repo.

37
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.880
And in doing that on our own repo, we started to

38
00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.120
learn it's not just about building a context,

39
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.280
but building loops on top of that context, because any context you build

40
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.480
is going to start rotting almost straight away.

41
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.480
And the longer you leave it untouched,

42
00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.920
the more damage that it might actually cause to your agent over time.

43
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.880
And so by building self-improvement loops on top of context

44
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.840
loops that look at the context check when the last time it was updated

45
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.080
evaluated to see if it's still the context

46
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.080
is still relevant for the repo, for the code, for the

47
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:55.040
the work that the agent is doing, but also observing the code

48
00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.640
that is being generated, the PR that are being merged and checking

49
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.680
if they are correct, if they are starting to fail,

50
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.680
if there are signs that things are starting to go wrong.

51
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.480
By observing these things, you can then act on them and go back to the repo.

52
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.840
Go back to the skills and improve that context.

53
00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.680
And this self-improvement loop means your skills don't go stale.

54
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.880
Your agents always have the best possible context

55
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.440
and therefore are producing the highest quality code.

56
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:23.680
And what we found

57
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.800
when doing this for ourselves over the course of a long period of time,

58
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:32.280
is that a collection of loops that build on top of each other

59
00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.440
and actually feed into each other and work on your SDLC

60
00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.720
is essentially a factory, but one that's tuned to your repo

61
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.720
and your organization the way you and your team work.

62
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.200
And so Tessl's building not just tools for managing context,

63
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.480
but also increasingly managing loops.

64
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.440
And where we're getting to is composing and building your factory as well.

65
00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.000
Now if we look at the software development lifecycle

66
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.280
from issue creation to production level code, as I mentioned,

67
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.680
there are multiple different loops in that flow that we can think about.

68
00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.800
I'm not going to go through them today.

69
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.560
We're going to focus on one particular loop,

70
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.840
which occurs at the end of that flow, which is the code review improvement loop.

71
00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.360
Now, code review is arguably one of the most essential steps

72
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.920
in a factory, because it doesn't matter

73
00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:30.720
what your agent is doing in code creation, if it's producing low quality code

74
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.960
that is unable to be approved

75
00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.760
by an agent, you can have to have a human come in and clean up the mess.

76
00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.400
The.

77
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.080
And when humans are coming in to clean up the mess, then you're turning software

78
00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.160
engineers into full time reviewers and nobody wants to do that.

79
00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:51.440
So the more we can get agent code review to be useful and to catch those issues,

80
00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.280
but also to feed back into the factory to make it better so those issues

81
00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.200
don't surface in the first place.

82
00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.240
That's the job we're trying to do here.

83
00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.480
And so that's what we're going to be talking about today.

